  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper and appendices are present; no material truncation is apparent.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "It compares held-out language-modeling loss across different recurrent architectures, generally at matched effective depth, but does not reproduce or fit the original scaling-law method.",
    "gate_note": "The study jointly increases model size and token budget using a Chinchilla-style allocation, but does not fit power laws, test extrapolation from smaller runs, or sweep compute-optimal parameter/token allocations. Its architecture comparisons bear on V4; the scaling, downstream-accuracy, backbone-transfer, and training-regime results do not test the specified power-law or predictability claims.",
    "test_reason": "This is an architecture paper. It proposes AsymLoop, a looped encoder–decoder Transformer, and reports its validation perplexity against Dense and Full Loop baselines. It also ablates AsymLoop's own components. It borrows Chinchilla's ~20 tokens per parameter only to set token budgets. It fits no power law and runs no extrapolation or fixed-compute allocation sweep. Nor does it sweep depth and width at fixed parameter count for a standard Transformer. The comparisons are the authors' method against baselines, and they differ in parameter count (e.g. 91.8M vs 95.4M) and in compute (+9–15% FLOPs). Looped and weight-tied models also vary compute per parameter, which V4's fixed-parameter shape claim does not cover. So none of its results tests a version. The reader's V4 link and its limits_scope labels should be dropped.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that AsymLoop gives lower validation perplexity and higher average downstream accuracy than depth-matched Full Loop from 60M to 1B. They report that the gains extend to an OLMo-style backbone, sparse MoE and GSM8K fine-tuning. They also note that AsymLoop costs more FLOPs than Full Loop but still beats a FLOP-matched widened Full Loop.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This paper studies an asymmetric recurrent architecture and compares its validation losses with alternative architectures; it does not use the original scaling-law fitting or extrapolation recipe.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Architecture and context-access design; recurrent depth; model scale; backbone type; sparse-MoE setting.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In looped/recurrent Transformers, recurrence topology and context-access design change validation loss at roughly matched parameters and effective depth. The changes are modest at 60M–350M and shrink at 1B (PPL 8.91 vs 8.86). OLMo-style: PPL 33.16 vs 28.25 (14.8%). Loop topology thus matters more at small scale than the shape-insensitivity of standard Transformers would suggest.",
        "proposed_claim": "In weight-tied looped Transformers, the recurrence topology (which layers loop, how context is accessed) changes held-out loss at matched effective depth, with the gap shrinking at larger scale.",
